WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.140
it's Michelle the muffin behind lab
muffin beauty science and today we're

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:10.710
going to be talking about SPF. SPF is a
phrase you'll hear thrown around a lot

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:20.300
when talking about sunscreens but what
exactly does it mean? Let's break it down

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.619
SPF stands for Sun Protection factor.
It's a number that you'll find on the

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:32.570
label of every officially sold sunscreen
usually on the front in really big font

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:41.219
How do they come up with this number?
SPF testing is done on actual human

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:45.239
volunteers in a laboratory. Two
milligrams of sunscreen per square

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.219
centimeter of skin is applied on to the
volunteers. A UV lamp is shone onto

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:55.050
Bare skin and sunscreened skin and the times
required for redness or erythema are

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.390
measured, and then fed into this formula.
You divide how long it took for the skin

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.159
to go red with the sunscreen by how long
it took for the skin to go red without

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:11.520
the sunscreen and the number you get is
the SPF. MED stands for the minimal

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.700
erythema dose in other words how much UV
was required for the skin to turn red.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.270
For example if a volunteer normally gets
burned in five minutes under the lamp

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.310
with bare skin, a sunscreen that stops
them from burning for 75 minutes or 15

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.930
times longer would be classified as SPF
15.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:36.270
So SPF tells you how many more times UV
your skin can handle before burning with

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:43.420
the sunscreen on compared to bare skin.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.020
So SPF tells you how much longer you can
stay in the sun with the sunscreen on...

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:54.920
doesn't it? Well no. This is a really
common misconception. While the length of

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.030
time before going red
is used in SPF testing with UV lamps, it

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:04.700
doesn't work this way in the real world.
The UV lamps used in testing put out a

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.310
consistent amount of UV so it's
proportional to time but in the real

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.900
world the intensity of the UV radiation
from the Sun that causes sunburn, known

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:21.020
as erythemal UV, changes a lot throughout
the day. In Sydney during summer here's

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.540
what the UV levels look like. This is a
graph of the UV index which shows you

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.600
the levels of erythermal UV. The higher
the UV index the more erythemal UV there

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:38.030
is. At midday the UV level is 11.9
which is over ten times the level

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.030
at 7:00 a.m. which is 0.9 so if your
bare skin normally gets burned after 20

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:50.360
minutes in the Sun at 7 a.m., at midday
you'd only last 22.7 minutes even with

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:57.560
SPF 15 sunscreen on, not 15 times 20
which is 300 minutes or 5 hours. That's a

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:03.650
massive difference. Even with SPF 50
sunscreen you'd only last 75 minutes at

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:09.650
midday. Most of the time it will be even
more complicated than this: clouds absorb

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:14.510
UV and some parts of reflective surfaces
like snow and water can actually double

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:20.360
your UV exposure.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:25.980
Here's another complication the SPF of
your sunscreen depends on the amount of

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.420
sunscreen you apply. As I mentioned
before the SPF of the sunscreen is

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:36.030
measured at two milligrams per square
centimeter on skin. Now 2 milligrams per

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.350
square centimeter doesn't sound like
that much but a square centimeter is

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:44.460
pretty small.
Most people use much less sunscreen than

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:50.160
this. Even when they're told to apply a
lot it translates to about 35 mL for

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.570
an adult human body which is around a
shot glass full. Most people end up

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:59.930
applying between a quarter and a half of
this recommended amount.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:06.980
According to a physical relationship
called the Beer-Lambert law, protection

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.750
should decrease exponentially with less
sunscreen so applying half the amount of

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.100
sunscreen would give you less than half
the amount of protection. But luckily

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:21.200
skin is a bit weird so microscopically
it's quite bumpy and the relationship

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.240
that most studies have found is close to
linear. This means that using half of the

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.080
recommended amount of sunscreen would
actually give you about a third or a

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:40.730
half of the labeled protection. There's a
common myth that says SPF 15 blocks 93%

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:50.330
of UV, SPF 30 blocks around 97% and SPF
50 blocks 98%, so SPF 50 would only give

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:57.350
5% more protection than SPF 15 and only
1% more protection than SPF 30. However

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:02.870
this isn't a good interpretation for a
number of reasons. Firstly these

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.950
percentages are only if you apply the
full amount of sunscreen: two milligrams

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:13.340
per square centimeter and as I mentioned
earlier most people don't. Secondly it's

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:17.060
more important to think about the UV
that does pass through the sunscreen and

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:22.040
gets to your skin rather than the UV
that doesn't pass through. So a better

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.530
way of looking at this: assuming that you
apply the recommended amount is that SPF

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:36.980
15 lets in 7% of UV SPF 30 less than 3%
and SPF 50 lets in 2%. So this

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:44.680
means SPF 15 lets in almost four times
as much UV as SPF 50. Here's an analogy:

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.090
you earn an average Australians income
$85000 a year. In

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:59.210
other words you don't earn 99.99999% of Australia's GDP 99.99999%

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:04.610
of Australia's GDP does not
enter your bank account. Your boss tells

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.710
you that an extra 1% of Australia's GDP
will enter your bank account if you work

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:15.590
an extra hour a week.
You say no because it's only 1%. You then

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:20.570
go off do some calculations and then
kick yourself because it's 17,000

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:26.470
million dollars which would make your
income 20 million percent higher. A

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:31.660
recent study found that SPF 100 plus
sunscreen did in fact in practice

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.360
protect more than SPF 50 sunscreen from
sunburns.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:42.590
So yes going higher with SPF isn't going
to be unnoticeable. In this study 199

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:47.930
people were told to apply two sunscreens,
one on each side of their face. They

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:53.200
didn't know which sunscreen was higher
SPF. After around six hours in thesun

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.680
scientists looked at how red the
people's faces were. More than half the

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.930
people were more sunburnt on the SPF 50+ side while only 5% of people were

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:08.150
more sunburnt on the SPF 100+ side.
There's been a few other studies as well

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:13.100
that have had similar results. The
problem with going too high with SPF

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.960
is that high SPF generally means more
sunscreen actives, which usually means

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.000
more greasy and more expensive products.
This might mean you won't end up

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.330
applying quite as much of that product
as you would with a lower protection

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.980
product, and so you'd end up with around
the same amount of protection. So I would

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:35.030
recommend going with the highest SPF
sunscreen that you can considering your

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.890
budget and the formula. There's also the
fact that with higher SPF

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:44.510
some people end up applying less
sunscreen or reapplying sunscreen less

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:50.150
often because they think they're more
protected but you still need to reapply

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.890
high SPF sunscreen regularly, because the
sunscreen film will wear off and wear

00:07:54.890 --> 00:08:00.069
down over time.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:05.300
As well as SPF
there's also UVAPF which is protection

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.649
from the longer wavelengths of UV that
don't cause burning. But they still cause

00:08:09.649 --> 00:08:15.199
skin damage and in particular this is
aging and pigmentation. UVA is also tends

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:19.399
to penetrate more deeply into the skin.
To ensure protection against UVA

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:24.759
you can either look for a numerical PPD
or PA rating which works a lot like SPF.

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:29.479
You can also look for the words broad
spectrum or the UVA logo on your

00:08:29.479 --> 00:08:33.589
sunscreen which means that the UVA
protection factor is at least one third

00:08:33.589 --> 00:08:37.339
of the labeled SPF. In some countries
like Australia

00:08:37.339 --> 00:08:42.769
if the sunscreen has a certain SPF it's
mandatory to be also broad-spectrum. It's

00:08:42.769 --> 00:08:45.949
important to remember that sunscreen
isn't the only type of sun protection

00:08:45.949 --> 00:08:50.329
and it isn't even the most effective
type of sun protection. You should be

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:54.170
avoiding sun exposure as much as
possible and also using different types

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:58.730
of protection like hats clothes and
sunglasses. These end up being more

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.389
effective than sunscreen because you
can't under apply them they won't wear

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:06.860
off and you don't need to remember to
reapply them. If you want to learn more

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.089
about sunscreen I have a few other
videos about sunscreen I talk about

00:09:11.089 --> 00:09:15.889
how much you need to apply, how to fit
sunscreen into a routine and what the

00:09:15.889 --> 00:09:20.360
difference is between chemical and
physical sunscreen ingredients. The links

